1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,169
Part three: the five animals. Each section of your board shows an animal and the pattern of stones it wants.

2
00:00:06,394 --> 00:00:13,794
The Rabbit wants three different colors. A complete set of three is worth six points, but an incomplete Rabbit scores nothing.

3
00:00:14,019 --> 00:00:23,485
The Turtle wants stones that are all the same color. The more you give, the more it pays: one, three, six, nine, and fourteen points for five stones.

4
00:00:23,710 --> 00:00:33,827
The Salmon wants one of each color, so every stone must be different. One stone is worth one point, then two, four, six, nine, and thirteen points for all six colors.

5
00:00:34,052 --> 00:00:46,119
The Owl wants pairs of the same color. Each complete pair is worth four points in your edition. Some English printings say three. You may start a new pair before finishing another, but only complete pairs score.

6
00:00:46,344 --> 00:00:55,950
The Beaver wants three of a kind and a same-colored pair. The triple is worth five, the pair is worth two, and completing both earns a seven point bonus, for fourteen in total.

7
00:00:56,175 --> 00:01:10,460
Clear Spirit Stones can only go on an animal's bonus space. They double that animal's points. Here, four red stones on the Turtle are worth nine, doubled to eighteen. But placing a Spirit Stone closes the section: no more stones can go there.

8
00:01:10,685 --> 00:01:21,997
A tip: a Spirit Stone is best on an animal that is already full or almost full. Doubling zero is still zero. And remember, every Spirit Stone out of the bag brings the end of the game closer.

9
00:01:22,222 --> 00:01:33,477
At the end, add up every animal. Here Ana has a doubled Rabbit for twelve, six for the Turtle, two for the Salmon, four for one Owl pair, and five for the Beaver's triple. That's 29 points.
